Having now shown clearly that the resurrection of the body is a complete restoration and reorganization of the physical system of man;

and that the elements of which his body is composed are eternal in their duration;

and that they form the tabernacle—the everlasting habitation of that spirit which animated them in this life;

and that the spirits and bodies of men are of equal importance and destined to form an eternal and inseparable union with each other; we must now return to our research as to the final destiny of the earth and its productions of animal and vegetable life.

We have already shown that the earth itself, and all its productions were deeply affected by the fall, and by the sins of the children of men; that the atonement which was made by Jesus Christ was not only for man, but also for the earth and all the fulness thereof; that all things were redeemed from the fall, and would finally be restored from all the dreadful effects thereof; and be regenerated, sanctified and renewed after the pattern, and in the likeness and image of its first creation; partaking of the same beauty, glory, excellence and perfection it had in the beginning.

But it is evident that this restitution did not take place at the first advent of the Messiah;

and that it has not taken place at at any time since;

therefore it is yet future, and must be fulfilled at a certain time which is appointed by infinite wisdom.

This certain time is called in holy writ "the times of restitution of all things which God hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began."Now this restitution is to be accomplished by nothing short of a second advent of the

Messiah.

He must again descend from heaven to earth in like manner as he ascended.

P. P. Pratt.

Philosophy finds no difficulty in triumphing over past and future ills, but present ills triumph over her.

SERMONS AND WRITINGS OF THE PROPHETS.

.Some of the most wonderful trees of this species in the world, are found on the Mount of Olives.

On the summit of the mountain is a square plot of ground, enclosed by a rough wall of stones, where are eight enormous olive trees of very great antiquity; the people 6f the neighborhood say that they were there at the beginning of our era, and their site is that of the "Garden of Gethsemane,"over the brook Kedron, where Jesus often resorted.

The trunks of these trees are of great size, they have become splintered and shrivelled with age, and are great curiosities as vegetable productions.

The olive, with the best cultivation, becomes a tree forty feet high.

The oil is contained in the pulp, not as in other fruits, in the nut or kernel.

To extract this the olives are first bruised by a millstone, then put into a sack, and then into the trough of a press for the purpose, which, by means of turning a strong screw, forces the stronger juice out, which is called virgin oil.

This is received in vessels half filled with water, from which it is taken off and set apart in earthen jars.

Several coarser kinds are obtained afterward by adding hot water to the bruised fruit.

The olive is cultivated south • as far as Cairo, and north to the middle of France.

The Chinese olive bears very sweet flowers, with which tea is flavored.

The broad-leaved American olive grown in Virginia, is from thirty-five to forty feet high.

Its wood is hard, its fruit palatable, and the flowers very sweet.

England is too cold for the olive, though sometimes the ripening fruit may be seen on a sunny southern wall in Devonshire.

Pliny tells us that oil warms the body and cools the head.

The ancients applied olive oil externally for health, for development and for luxury, always, before their baths and before any athletic exertion.

Oil externally applied is now becoming popular for dyspeptic and consumptive patients.

Many are said to be benefited by an inunction of cod-liver oil.

While one physician denounces what another prescribes, we sometimes turn hopelessly to nature's simplest aids and find them best.

Who shall tell why faith and anointing in cathedral and palace, in birth and death, are ever side by side?

Why upon the heads of kings and prophets has the oil been poured?

Why is the oil of joy and the oil of gladness promised in the deepest sorrow?

May these not be symbolic of some healing virtue not yet fully understood?

Egyptian, Jew, Greek, Roman, Arab,

African and Esquimaux have all used oil externally as invigorating, strengthening and protecting.

In baptism, in confirmation, in ordination, in consecration, in benediction, in coronation, in tradition and legend the oil of olives has kept its hallowed place.

An interest both historical and classical gathers around the beautiful tree whose leaves have been a symbol of peace and blessing ever since the dove brought to the storm-tossed ark the peaceful olive-leaf.

—Selected.

PRIDE IN WORK.

There is an old saying, which is thus expressed, "Anything which is worth doing at all, is worth doing well."In this proverb there is a truth which may be amplified at will in any given direction; indeed, it is only according to the manner in which things are done that we can estimate men's appreciation of their duty or calling in life.

Much of what men do is performed in a loose or mechanical way;

there is no vim in their effort, no snap in their method, no inspiration in their mood, no pride in bringing to pass the highest ideal or best possible result.

If we should award them any ambition—if they are possessed of any—it is to get through with the work on hand, something with the feeling expressed during a local season of so-called "reformation."After the ef-

PRIDE IN WORK.

2.^1 fort had exhausted itself, one was heard to say, "Thank the Lord we are through with that!"unheeding the fact, established in the nature of things, that successful, mental or spiritual reformation is no superficial or spasmodic feeling;

it is a real work, involving much reflection and continuous activity, reaching out from point to point, advancing step by step, noting, with resolute purpose, all achievement, and using present success as an aid and index of progress in the future.

A man would not be considered a good farmer if his land was left to the wantonness of every common weed;

he would not be considered a thorough farmer if his labors were confined to the centre of his fields and every corner and fence line given up to rubbish and riotous or noxious vegetation.

Or if the land was cultivated with fair ability, would his reputation be of the best if gates and fences were broken down, if his work teams or cattle, his horned stock or even his poultry showed his common neglect;

tools, machinery, wagons, blistering in the summer's sun or soaking in the autumn rains, would be a merited rebuke to his claim for consideration as a model farmer—a man taking pride in his work.

The hoe or spade in the ditch, the plow in the furrows, the harrow by the way side, the povertystricken animal, the uncultivated corner, the broken fence, the unopened ditch, or drain, would only be so many reminders, uttering their silent but significant protest against the intelligent professor of the divine art of fundamental agriculture.

The woman who aspires to be a true wife, a real helpmeet, would be only half a success if she was but a clever caterer to the liking of her Lord, in the every day matter of food; her soup might be unexceptionable in flavor; her roast always done to a turn, her potatoes mealy, her bread light and her butter good, yet dirty dishes and soiled table cloth, and disorderly arrangement would nullify in many all the real good;

if the domestic rule of woman was only exercised in one direction, where would be the enjoyment or realization of an ideal home;

if cleanliness were confined to the centre of a room, if under the beds and in the closets and places oft unseen there was an accumulation of domestic debris;

if ashes, and dish water, and soap suds, and soiled linen, if lack of order, method, and arrangement were every where visible, who would believe that the queen had pride in rule, or possessed any claim to recognition as a clever, painstaking and indisputably successful housewife?

Any mechanic who is without pride in his labor will forever remain a botch, or if otherwise, will be so only because of supervision;

left to himself his work will be slighted;

there might be superficially an appearance of perfection, but in detail it would be as occurred in the first foundation of Salt Lake Temple, lacking that solidity and thoroughness, which would have been fatal to that edifice;

and so constrained President Young, who was one who took pride in good work, to have the most of it torn up and relaid in a more thorough manner.

We have all heard of men, who were designated handy men, "jack of all trades,"but this was generally coupled with the conclusion that they were "master of none;"now one possessing a proper pride in his work will never want only the best;

if he cannot reach it by patience, industry and perseverance, he will endeavor to approximate as close as is practicable, and in his experience with mediocrity in himself or others, he will never forget the saying of the wise man, that "there is always room higher up;"to that position, however, no idler will ever reach, no indifferent or self contented one will soar;

"excelsior"must be on the banner, and on the lips, and in the heart of all who crave for and are determined on success.

Men's faculties are sharpened by use, their powers are developed by exercise, their ability grows by effort, and final triumph comes of trifles daily or hourly overcome; the conqueror's wreath only falls of right to the one who has struggled and fought on many a stormy battle field.

Often where there has appeared to be the divine afflatus of inspiration, the unquestioned power of genius, could

2 3 2 PRIDE IN WORK.

we peer below the surface, we should only find the unappreciated force of honest pride in work.

What a grand thing it is that this pride brings with it its own exceeding great reward;

a well turned furrow, an even field of grain, a thick set meadow, a well broke colt, a clean and thrifty farm, a well appointed home, sunshine around the table, peace in the family, content with labor, well directed mechanical skill and activity, are each the fruits of that pluck which aims at, if it find unreached, the acme of confessed perfection.

The argument is good in other directions, for there are those who expect to be scholars, yet take no pride in topic, or study, or books;

they are without a hobby, without tools and without purpose; say they, it is nice to be intelligent it is good to be educated, it is desirable (in a mild way) to accumulate knowledge, but the game is hardly worth the powder and shot, the benefits are hardly equal to the outlay, or the interest to investment;

as if the possession of understanding and the development of mental ability and grasp could be counted or weighed in a balance as the mints do the coin of the realm.

No truth is more potent in the regions of spirituality and religious growth than this self same pride in work;

for there are Latter-day Saints who are so mechanical, they are like the door upon its hinges, simply to come and go;

useful, no doubt, but unprogressive and almost destitute of life; they are victims to coldness and formality, without snap and interest in the life that now is, although full of hope in regard to the life beyond;

they believe that the heavens were opened to Joseph, yet they are as brass to them; they believe that angels ministered to some in this dispensation, but mentally this period is projected outward almost as far as the Apostolic age;

they believe, of course, in revelation, but its voice of music never swelled to them; they believe in spiritual gifts, but these have not given glow to their experience;

they believe in the power and influence of the Spirit, but no silent force of that peculiar agency has given life and verdure, bud and blossom or fruit to them;

in fact they are only Latter-day.Saints in name and theory, are without that inspirational energy which works in hope as the farmer works in his fields, the mechanic with his tools, or the housewife in her home.

There is theory but no practice, hope but no realization; an easy going kind of indolent satisfaction, but no bustling activity, no exuberant and overflowing vitality, hence there has been spiritual sickness and apostasy which is spiritual death;

such were born of the will of man;

it can hardly be said they were truly born of God; there may be probably an appearance of cultivation, of cleanliness, the centre of the field, the middle of the room, points most prominent for the observation of others, but there are unswept corners, broken fences wild sections, or the rank vegetation of unprofitable weeds, and outside show;

superficial restraint, local uncleanliness, tell that there is no pride in the great work and effort of seeking to be a real, living, thrifty, vigorous, powerful Latterday Saint.

The old Apostle said it was right to be always "zealously affected in a good cause;"and another said "whatsoever thy hand findeth to do (in righteousness) do it with thy might."It is the half hearted man, the careless shuffling professor, the dodging evader of work, who is destined to "shame and confusion of face.""Unstable as water they shall not excel;"but God will bless the quiet worker, He will be with the devoted man or woman, the angels will draw near to such, and the spirit will under every emergency comfort and succor those who from continued labor are weary or tired by the way.

The laborer proud of his work, taking interest in it, adapting himself to it, using all the created appliances which have been provided on his behalf, exercising meanwhile faith and trust "in Him who hath promised,"and while seeking to keep the commandments invoking the promised gift of the Holy Ghost must and will become "a Latterday Saint,"a successful seeker and
